WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.108 --> 00:00:12.245
teamLab started in 2001
as an art collective.

00:00:12.245 --> 00:00:15.515
I created it as a place
for various experts

00:00:15.515 --> 00:00:18.118
to come together and experiment.

00:00:19.419 --> 00:00:23.323 position:56%
Those experts range from
international artists and architects

00:00:23.323 --> 00:00:25.392
to programmers and mathematicians

00:00:25.392 --> 00:00:28.028
seeking to navigate
the convergence of art,

00:00:28.028 --> 00:00:30.263 position:56%
science, tech and the natural world.

00:00:30.597 --> 00:00:32.999
I launched it while I was
in grad school.

00:00:32.999 --> 00:00:37.999
I studied sciences, specializing
in physics and mathematics.

00:00:40.373 --> 00:00:45.373
Like now, if you capture
the world through a lens

00:00:45.445 --> 00:00:49.382 position:56%
the screen sets the boundary between
your body and the cut out world.

00:00:49.382 --> 00:00:53.019 position:59%
The point of view is fixed and what to
focus on is already determined.

00:00:54.087 --> 00:00:57.090
To explore what it means
not to be constrained,

00:00:57.090 --> 00:01:00.760
Toshiyuki opened the world's
first digital art museum in Tokyo

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:03.763
aptly called teamLab Borderless.

00:01:03.763 --> 00:01:06.566
And he says advancements in
technology has been the key

00:01:06.566 --> 00:01:08.535
to greater freedom of expression.

00:01:10.236 --> 00:01:13.039
From experimenting with
interactive installations

00:01:13.039 --> 00:01:15.608
to having visitors step into water

00:01:15.608 --> 00:01:20.608 position:66%
teamLab has wowed people
all over the world in more than 50 cities.

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:25.880
When we hinge our artistic
expressions on physical items,

00:01:26.686 --> 00:01:29.055
it's difficult to show change.

00:01:29.055 --> 00:01:34.027
I think when we free ourselves
from those material items,

00:01:34.027 --> 00:01:37.597
we can express the act of change
and even the flow of time itself.

00:01:37.597 --> 00:01:40.166
One of its most tucked
away museums can be found

00:01:40.166 --> 00:01:42.702
in a forest in southern Japan.

00:01:42.702 --> 00:01:46.306
teamLab's Digitized Nature
project explores how digital tech

00:01:46.306 --> 00:01:50.043
can turn nature into
art without harming it.

00:01:50.043 --> 00:01:53.346
This forest has been here
for a really long time.

00:01:53.346 --> 00:01:55.748
The garden was created
back in the Edo period.

00:01:55.748 --> 00:01:58.852 position:61%
It's a historic place where people have
lived from generation to generation

00:01:58.852 --> 00:02:02.455
and has accumulated much meaning.

00:02:02.455 --> 00:02:06.826 position:59%
I want people to come here to feel the

00:02:06.826 --> 00:02:11.826
long continuity of life
through our various artworks.

00:02:13.133 --> 00:02:15.935
teamLab has no plans to slow down,

00:02:15.935 --> 00:02:18.171 position:58%
set to open a new museum in Abu Dhabi

00:02:18.171 --> 00:02:20.907
focused on the concept of
environmental phenomena,

00:02:20.907 --> 00:02:23.910
slated to open in 2024.

00:02:24.477 --> 00:02:27.147
Toshiyuki says digital
technology has made it possible

00:02:27.147 --> 00:02:29.382
for artworks to expand physically,

00:02:29.382 --> 00:02:33.686
which allows for manipulating
and using much larger spaces.

00:02:33.686 --> 00:02:36.456
That way, viewers are able
to experience the artwork

00:02:36.456 --> 00:02:38.525
much more directly,

00:02:38.525 --> 00:02:41.060
now and in the future.

